#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run the tests a change can affect: a helper for the lane, not a gate.

    tools/gate --suggest                 # the targeted set, read statically (no Buck2)
    tools/gate                           # run the targeted set
    tools/gate --tests <test>...         # run exactly these (the forms below)
    tools/gate --base <commit> ...       # the change from <commit>'s merge base (default origin/main)
    tools/gate --full                    # every stage: main's hourly run (tools/main-runs), or a brief's explicit opt-in
    tools/gate --bless-perf-counts       # regenerate the reviewed instruction budgets

The targeted set is what the change can affect (impact.py, gating.Selection):
the test targets of every crate whose source changed and the tests that read
any other changed file; every changed or added corpus case with its twins and
their agreement variants; the corpus cases, packages and examples whose import
closure includes a changed std or package module; the native programs a
changed backend or runtime file affects, on both backends; the gate tooling's
tests and the models' checks when they changed; and for a compiler crate (one
the corpus campaigns run through: front end, checker, lowering, validator,
interpreter, engine, driver; impact.CRATE_CAMPAIGNS) the smoke slice: one
corpus case per rule ID and expectation kind, read from the corpus
(impact.smoke_slice), with its agreement variants, about 5 CPU-minutes.

Everything runs in one Buck2 invocation (the host checks in parallel), and
each failure prints the commands that rerun it alone. Failures listed in
origin/main's tools/known-failures.tsv (test id, reason, owner lane, date) are
excluded, and each exclusion is printed with its row. Nothing is recorded:
tools/land needs no receipt, and checks only what breaks everyone.

No lane runs a whole conformance or agreement campaign, and main moving never
calls for a rerun: `tools/gate --full` runs hourly on origin/main
(tools/main-runs), which bisects each new red to the landing that caused it,
lists it as a known failure and tells that lane.

`--tests` takes:

    <test target>          a Buck2 test, `//:name` or `name`; a sharded binary's name runs every shard
    suggested              the targeted set
    smoke                  the corpus smoke slice and its agreement variants
    native-smoke           the native smoke, toolchain proof and two-host link gate
    conformance:<path>     the corpus cases under <path> (a case, or a directory prefix)
    agreement:<path>       those cases' variants in the seed-1 sample
    native:<program>       the LLVM smoke matrix and Cranelift parity for <program>
    all-tests              every Buck2 test

A change that touches a generated file (tools/buck2/generated.py: docs/api.md,
which only tools/land writes, or a gitignored build file) is refused before
anything runs, with the commands that drop it.

--full runs every stage, including the whole conformance, agreement and native
campaigns, the release profile, the full_gate_ tests and tools/check-native.sh,
except the stages tools/main-runs-skip.tsv lists (stage, reason, owner lane,
date); it reports every stage and fails after the last. tools/main-runs runs
it at pool priority HIRSEL_PRIORITY (40, below a lane's 50), with each stage's
Buck2 logs under HIRSEL_FULL_LOGS so it can read every target's result.
"""

